theory RatchetProtocol
/*
  Frappuccino STREAM -- V2 ephemeral-ratchet enrollment & authentication
  protocol, modelled in the symbolic (Dolev-Yao) setting.

  ROADMAP 8.4 item (5). Companion to the TLA+ FSM model (item 4):
    - TLA+    proves the *state-machine* discipline (monotone batch, use-once
              mask, anti-replay) exhaustively over the 50-slot counter space.
    - Tamarin (this file) proves the *cryptographic* protocol layer under an
              active network attacker (Dolev-Yao): unforgeable enrollment,
              authenticated challenge-response, batch-rotation lineage
              (no rogue batch), secrecy of ephemeral keys, and forward secrecy
              of past authentications.

  Source of truth:
    crypto-rs/core/src/ratchet.rs       (sign_and_advance, advance_batch / RotationProof)
    crypto-rs/core/src/identity.rs      (sign_once(message, domain) -- enrollment)
    crypto-rs/core/src/signature_domain.rs    (R-C-1: the explicit domain tags)
    crypto-rs/stream/src/protocol.rs    (enroll / challenge / verify / rotate-batch)
    server/app/routes/auth_v2.py        (the verifier: acceptance conditions)
    docs/invariants-ratchet-verification.md   (Partie 1 sections A / B / E)

  Abstraction (documented scope). A batch is modelled by ONE representative
  ephemeral slot key. The 50-slot multiplicity and the consumed-mask
  bookkeeping are the finite-state concern proven exhaustively by TLA+ (item 4);
  here every slot key is an independent Ed25519 key, which is exactly what the
  Dolev-Yao layer needs. A slot is consumed by EITHER a challenge-response auth
  OR a batch rotation, at most once -- the server's use-once enforcement
  (auth_v2.py: consume_ephemeral_key / "Signer key already consumed"). That
  sentence used to rest on an encoding artefact alone: SlotAvail is a LINEAR
  fact, so the server rules could only fire once per slot, but no lemma said so.
  It is now `slot_use_once` below, with a negative control (NC4) that makes
  SlotAvail persistent and requires the lemma to fall. The distinction became
  load-bearing on 2026-09-03: the client deliberately REPLAYS unconfirmed
  rotation proofs at the start of every authentication, so "an already-applied
  proof grants nothing" is something the design now leans on, not a nicety.

  Writing that lemma immediately showed the sentence above was too strong, so
  read the qualifier. Use-once is guaranteed for HONESTLY generated slots, and
  unless the slot was revealed. Server_Rotate installs the `newpk` it is handed
  ON THE WIRE, and neither this model nor the relay checks that the new batch
  excludes the signer's own key: `rotate_batch` overwrites `batch_keys` and
  resets `consumed_indices` to empty (ratchet_registry.py). An attacker who
  enrols an identity of their own can therefore rotate its slot TO ITSELF and
  re-arm it indefinitely. Tamarin found exactly that trace. It grants nothing
  over an identity they already own, which is why this is a scope qualifier on
  the lemma rather than a defect to fix.

  Second abstraction, and it cuts the other way. !DeviceSlot is PERSISTENT, so
  the modelled device can keep signing from a slot the real device has erased:
  `advance_batch` wipes the outgoing batch. This is sound for the security
  lemmas -- giving the honest device MORE power can only make them harder to
  prove -- but it means the availability failure the replay queue repairs is not
  expressible here. In this model a desync repairs itself by re-signing; on a
  real device the batch that could sign is gone. Availability is out of scope
  for Tamarin by design; see the TLA+ model and section 4.2 of the architecture
  document.

  Domain separation (R-C-1, commit da56da4). Every V2 signature commits to an
  explicit one-byte domain tag prepended to the signed message
  (core/src/signature_domain.rs, mirrored byte-for-byte by the verifier in
  server/app/signature_domain.py). The live contexts in this model:
      0x01 AuthChallenge  ephemeral slot signs nonce||ts   (/auth/v2/verify)
      0x02 BatchRotation  ephemeral slot signs concat(pks) (/auth/v2/rotate-batch)
      0x03 Enrollment     long-term  key  signs concat(pks) (/auth/v2/enroll)
  The ephemeral slot is dual-use (it signs AuthChallenge AND BatchRotation); the
  model's symbolic tags ('auth' / 'rotate') mirror those real wire tags. The
  model proves rotation safety genuinely DEPENDS on that ephemeral separation:
  collapsing the two ephemeral tags (NC2) re-falsifies rotation_authentic.

  Phase C relay-blind retired 0x04 ArchiveAuth: archive reads are now
  identity-free (the phrase-derived report_id is the capability), so the
  long-term key signs ONLY enrollment -- it is no longer dual-use. The earlier
  long-term cross-context attack surface (an archive signature replayed as a
  rogue enrollment -- the R-C-1 finding, demonstrated by the now-removed NC3) is
  therefore GONE by construction: with a single long-term context there is
  nothing to separate. root_authentic still holds; the 0x03 tag stays as defense
  in depth for any future long-term context.
  History: before R-C-1 the separation was only *implicit* in message length
  (40-byte nonce||ts vs 1600-byte concat) -- the audit's open finding; R-C-1
  made it explicit, and Phase C removed the long-term dual-use entirely.
*/

begin

builtins: signing

/* The server's many `if not verify(...): reject` checks are modelled by an
   action fact Eq(verify(...), true) constrained by this equality restriction:
   a trace is only valid where the verification actually succeeded. */
restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/* The real server rejects a second enrollment of an already-known identity
   (auth_v2.py enroll -> 409 "Identity already enrolled"). One root batch per
   identity. */
restriction EnrollOncePerIdentity:
  "All idpk e1 e2 #i #j.
      ServerEnroll(idpk, e1) @ #i & ServerEnroll(idpk, e2) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

/* ===========================================================================
   ENROLLMENT
   Device: fresh long-term identity ltk and the batch-0 slot e0. Publishes the
   identity pubkey, the slot pubkey, and the enrollment signature
   sign(pk(e0), ltk) -- i.e. EnrollmentKit.sign_once over concat(batch_0_pks),
   here represented by the single batch-0 slot pubkey.
   =========================================================================== */
rule Device_Enroll:
    [ Fr(~ltk), Fr(~e0) ]
  --[ Enroll(pk(~ltk), pk(~e0)), LtkKey(~ltk), SlotKey(~e0) ]->
    [ !Ltk(~ltk)
    , !DeviceSlot(~ltk, ~e0)
    , Out(pk(~ltk))
    , Out(pk(~e0))
    , Out(sign(<'enroll', pk(~e0)>, ~ltk)) ]

/* Server: verifies the enrollment signature under the claimed identity key,
   then records the batch-0 slot as the available (authorized) slot for this
   identity. RootAuthorized marks the base of the lineage chain. */
rule Server_Enroll:
    [ In(idpk), In(epk), In(sig) ]
  --[ ServerEnroll(idpk, epk)
    , Eq(verify(sig, <'enroll', epk>, idpk), true)
    , RootAuthorized(idpk, epk) ]->
    [ SlotAvail(idpk, epk) ]

/* ===========================================================================
   CHALLENGE  (POST /auth/challenge)
   A fresh server nonce, one-shot: the linear ServerNonce fact is consumed by
   exactly one verify (anti-replay). The server-stamped timestamp + the +/-30s
   skew window (auth_v2.py) only *tighten* an already one-shot nonce, so they
   are a replay-window refinement orthogonal to the symbolic model; omitted
   here and noted in docs/TAMARIN_RATCHET.md.
   =========================================================================== */
rule Server_Challenge:
    [ Fr(~n) ]
  --[ Challenge(~n) ]->
    [ Out(~n), ServerNonce(~n) ]

/* ===========================================================================
   CHALLENGE-RESPONSE AUTH  (POST /auth/v2/verify)
   Device signs the nonce with its current slot key; the wire message also
   carries the claimed identity (VerifyBody.ed25519_pk) which the server uses
   to look up the slot's batch. The SIGNATURE itself covers only the nonce
   (protocol.rs: message = nonce || ts) -- the identity binding is the registry
   lookup, not the signature. Server verifies under the slot pubkey, consumes
   the slot (use-once) and the nonce (one-shot), issues a JWT.
   =========================================================================== */
rule Device_AuthSign:
    [ !DeviceSlot(~ltk, ~e), In(n) ]
  --[ DeviceAuthSign(pk(~ltk), pk(~e), n) ]->
    [ Out(<pk(~ltk), pk(~e), n, sign(<'auth', n>, ~e)>) ]

rule Server_Verify:
    [ SlotAvail(idpk, epk), ServerNonce(n), In(<idpk, epk, n, sig>) ]
  --[ ServerAccept(idpk, epk, n)
    , Eq(verify(sig, <'auth', n>, epk), true)
    , ConsumeSlot(idpk, epk) ]->
    [ IssuedJWT(idpk, n) ]

/* ===========================================================================
   BATCH ROTATION  (POST /auth/v2/rotate-batch)
   Device signs the next batch's slot pubkey with its CURRENT slot key
   (advance_batch / RotationProof over concat(new_batch_pks)). The server
   verifies under the current authorized slot, consumes it, and installs the
   new slot as the authorized one. RotateAuthorized chains the lineage.
   =========================================================================== */
rule Device_Rotate:
    [ !DeviceSlot(~ltk, ~e), Fr(~e2) ]
  --[ DeviceRotate(pk(~ltk), pk(~e), pk(~e2)), SlotKey(~e2) ]->
    [ !DeviceSlot(~ltk, ~e2)
    , Out(<pk(~e), pk(~e2), sign(<'rotate', pk(~e2)>, ~e)>) ]

rule Server_Rotate:
    [ SlotAvail(idpk, signerpk), In(<signerpk, newpk, sig>) ]
  --[ ServerRotate(idpk, signerpk, newpk)
    , Eq(verify(sig, <'rotate', newpk>, signerpk), true)
    , RotateAuthorized(idpk, signerpk, newpk)
    , ConsumeSlot(idpk, signerpk) ]->
    [ SlotAvail(idpk, newpk) ]

/* ===========================================================================
   (Phase C relay-blind) The ARCHIVE-SCOPE AUTH flow is gone: archive reads are
   now identity-free (the phrase-derived report_id is the capability), so the
   long-term key no longer authenticates directly -- it signs only enrollment.
   The Device_ArchiveSign / Server_ArchiveAuth rules and the archive_auth_origin
   lemma were removed with it; see the domain-separation note in the header.
   =========================================================================== */

/* ===========================================================================
   ADVERSARY: state-compromise reveals (for forward-secrecy reasoning).
   Reveal_Slot leaks the device's CURRENT ephemeral slot secret (extraction of
   the on-device ratchet state); Reveal_Ltk leaks the long-term identity secret.
   =========================================================================== */
rule Reveal_Slot:
    [ !DeviceSlot(~ltk, ~e) ]
  --[ RevealSlot(pk(~e)) ]->
    [ Out(~e) ]

rule Reveal_Ltk:
    [ !Ltk(~ltk) ]
  --[ RevealLtk(pk(~ltk)) ]->
    [ Out(~ltk) ]

// ===========================================================================
// LEMMAS
// ===========================================================================

/* --- Sanity / executability: the honest flows are reachable at all. -------- */
lemma auth_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex idpk epk n #i. ServerAccept(idpk, epk, n) @ #i"

lemma rotation_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex idpk s nw #i. ServerRotate(idpk, s, nw) @ #i"

/* --- (Part 1 A/B) Secrecy of ephemeral slot keys. -------------------------- */
/* The adversary never learns an honest ephemeral slot secret unless that
   slot's state was explicitly revealed. The device only ever emits pk(e) and
   signatures sign(_, e), so e itself stays secret. */
lemma slotkey_secrecy:
  "All e #i #k.
      SlotKey(e) @ #i & K(e) @ #k ==> (Ex #r. RevealSlot(pk(e)) @ #r)"

/* --- Secrecy of the long-term identity key. -------------------------------- */
lemma ltk_secrecy:
  "All l #i #k.
      LtkKey(l) @ #i & K(l) @ #k ==> (Ex #r. RevealLtk(pk(l)) @ #r)"

/* --- (Part 1 B/E) Authentication: a JWT is issued only against a genuine
   signature by the holder of the slot key, unless that slot was compromised.
   Authentication is on the SLOT KEY (the signature covers only the nonce); the
   identity attribution idpk2 may differ from idpk under an unknown-key-share
   re-registration, which transfers no authority because the nonce is one-shot
   and only the slot holder can sign -- see docs/TAMARIN_RATCHET.md. ----------- */
lemma auth_slot_origin [reuse]:
  "All idpk epk n #i.
      ServerAccept(idpk, epk, n) @ #i
      & (Ex e #s. SlotKey(e) @ #s & epk = pk(e))
      ==> (Ex idpk2 #j. DeviceAuthSign(idpk2, epk, n) @ #j & #j < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealSlot(epk) @ #r & #r < #i)"

/* --- (Part 1 C/E) Anti-replay: a server challenge nonce authenticates at most
   one session. The linear ServerNonce fact is consumed by the first accept. -- */
lemma nonce_use_once:
  "All idpk1 epk1 idpk2 epk2 n #i #j.
      ServerAccept(idpk1, epk1, n) @ #i & ServerAccept(idpk2, epk2, n) @ #j
      ==> #i = #j"

/* --- OPEN ITEM: the rotation flow's use-once property is NOT proven here, and
   the abstraction paragraph at the top of this file has been overstating it for
   as long as this model has existed. This is a documented gap, not an omission.

   The claim is "a slot is consumed by EITHER an auth OR a rotation, at most
   once". `ConsumeSlot` is emitted by both server rules and appears in NO lemma;
   the property holds only because SlotAvail is a LINEAR fact. Make it persistent
   and all ten lemmas still verify while the server rotates one slot forever.

   Three attempts to state it were written and all three were FALSIFIED by
   Tamarin (2026-09-04), which is what makes this worth recording:

     1. Unconditional               -> falsified, 16 steps
     2. + (Ex #r. RevealSlot(s))    -> falsified, 15 steps
     3. + RevealLtk, then restricted to honest slots via SlotKey, as
        `auth_slot_origin` does                              -> still falsified

   The concrete counter-example is ROTATE-TO-SELF. Enrolment is open by design,
   so an attacker enrols an identity of their own and submits a rotation whose
   new batch contains the signer's own key: `sign(<'rotate', pk(x)>, x)` with
   newpk == signerpk. Server_Rotate consumes SlotAvail(idpk, x) and immediately
   reinstalls the identical fact, so that key is consumable again, indefinitely.

   THE RELAY DOES THE SAME. `rotate_batch` writes the submitted keys verbatim and
   resets `consumed_indices` to empty, without checking that the signer's key is
   absent from the new batch (server/app/ratchet_registry.py). Rotating a slot to
   itself re-arms it.

   Is it a defect? Not on its own: reaching it requires an authorized, unconsumed
   slot of the target identity, so the attacker already holds what the slot would
   grant, and doing it to your own identity gains nothing. What is wrong today is
   the DOCUMENTATION, which promises a use-once guarantee the model does not
   deliver -- and it became load-bearing on 2026-09-03, when the client started
   deliberately replaying unconfirmed rotation proofs at each authentication.
   That replay is safe, but its safety currently rests on an encoding accident
   rather than on a proof.

   To close this properly one of two things has to happen: either the relay
   refuses a new batch containing the signer's key (a server change, and then the
   unconditional lemma becomes provable), or the lemma is stated over a notion of
   honest identity this model does not currently carry. Neither is a five-minute
   edit, which is why this is an open item rather than a bent lemma. */

/* --- (Part 1 B/E) Rotation authentication: an accepted batch rotation whose
   signer slot was honestly generated was actually produced by the genuine
   device (DeviceRotate), unless that slot's key was revealed. This is the
   unforgeability of the custom RotationProof (advance_batch): the signature
   sign(newpk, slot) cannot be produced without the slot's private key, so an
   active attacker cannot inject a forged batch rotation. --------------------- */
lemma rotation_authentic:
  "All idpk signerpk newpk #i.
      ServerRotate(idpk, signerpk, newpk) @ #i
      & (Ex e #s. SlotKey(e) @ #s & signerpk = pk(e))
      ==> (Ex idpk2 #j. DeviceRotate(idpk2, signerpk, newpk) @ #j & #j < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealSlot(signerpk) @ #r & #r < #i)"

/* --- (Part 1 B/E) Rotation lineage: every accepted rotation was signed by a
   slot that was itself already authorized -- at enrollment (the root) or by a
   prior rotation. By induction this chains every batch back to the root. No
   rogue batch can be injected without an authorized predecessor. ------------- */
lemma rotation_lineage [use_induction]:
  "All idpk signerpk newpk #i.
      RotateAuthorized(idpk, signerpk, newpk) @ #i
      ==> (Ex #j. RootAuthorized(idpk, signerpk) @ #j & #j < #i)
        | (Ex prev #j. RotateAuthorized(idpk, prev, signerpk) @ #j & #j < #i)"

/* --- Base anchor of the lineage: a root authorization implies a genuine
   enrollment by the identity owner, unless its long-term key was revealed.
   Composed with rotation_lineage: every authorized batch of an honest,
   un-revealed identity is rooted at that device's own enrollment. ------------ */
lemma root_authentic:
  "All idpk epk #i.
      RootAuthorized(idpk, epk) @ #i
      & (Ex ltk #s. LtkKey(ltk) @ #s & idpk = pk(ltk))
      ==> (Ex #j. Enroll(idpk, epk) @ #j & #j < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealLtk(idpk) @ #r & #r < #i)"

/* --- (Part 1 B) Forward secrecy: an authentication accepted while its slot is
   not (yet) compromised is genuine, even if that slot's state is revealed
   LATER. Extracting the device state at time T cannot retroactively forge a
   past authentication -- the "can only forge remaining slots, never past
   uploads" guarantee from ratchet.rs, per independent ephemeral keys. -------- */
lemma forward_secrecy_auth:
  "All idpk epk n #i.
      ServerAccept(idpk, epk, n) @ #i
      & (Ex e #s. SlotKey(e) @ #s & epk = pk(e))
      & (All #r. RevealSlot(epk) @ #r ==> #i < #r)
      ==> (Ex idpk2 #j. DeviceAuthSign(idpk2, epk, n) @ #j & #j < #i)"

/* (Phase C relay-blind) archive_auth_origin removed: the archive-scope flow no
   longer exists (identity-free reads). The long-term key now signs only
   enrollment, so its cross-context replay surface -- the R-C-1 finding this
   lemma + NC3 demonstrated -- is gone by construction (root_authentic still
   holds; see the header domain-separation note). */

end
